feat(#69): give every agent the efficiency invariants automatically

Two related waste patterns from log analysis: (1) the rule about applying
efficiency-rules.md only ever reached the top-level agent's prompt, never a
sub-agent's, so 28 recorded sub-agent transcripts across 363 tool calls
never saw it; (2) CLAUDE.md pointed at AGENTS.md in prose only, so every
parent and sub-agent that needed it paid for an explicit read.

CLAUDE.md is the one channel documented as both inherited by sub-agents
(built-in Explore/Plan excepted) and re-read after compaction, so it's the
right place to fix both: `@AGENTS.md` makes that file load automatically
instead of via tool call, and a short "Sandcastle session invariants"
section inlines the handful of judgement-level rules from
efficiency-rules.md that no deterministic guard could express (duplicate
sub-agent work, over-scoping a sub-agent's context, re-verifying a
documented finding, patching around a bad invocation instead of fixing it,
and blocking review on a judgement call). The rules file stays canonical
for the long-form/mechanical rules; the inlined set deliberately doesn't
restate those.

Verified empirically that a sub-agent spawned mid-session reflects the
CLAUDE.md that was loaded at session start, not a live re-read — confirming
the issue's own premise that compaction (or a fresh session) is the only
reload point. That means this session's own sub-agent transcripts can't
show the new invariant text landing (the session's cached copy predates
this edit); the next real run, which loads CLAUDE.md fresh, is what
exercises the fix end to end.

Ran /code-review (Standards + Spec sub-agents) against
origin/epic/64-sandcastle-guardrails: no hard standards violations; fixed
two spec nits it raised (a redundant AGENTS.md pointer sitting next to the
new import, and a bullet that read as mechanically-guardable rather than
judgement-only).
EOF
